<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lord Mengchang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 279 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lord Mengchang, personal name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> Wen, was a prince and minister of the State of Qi in the late 4th and early 3rd centuries BCE, one of the Four Lords of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>. Holding the fief of Xue, he kept several thousand retainers, whose tricks, such as imitating a cock's crow to open a frontier gate, saved him from captivity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>. He served at times as chief minister of Qi, Qin and Wei.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lord-mengchang.jpg" alt="Lord Mengchang" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 冯梦龙、蔡元放 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%AD%9F%E5%B0%9D%E5%90%9B.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
